.TH DANBY-TUNNEL 1
.SH NAME
danby-tunnel \- getting through a wall you have not the energy to climb
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/tunnel
.SH MODEL
A particle meets a rectangular barrier of height V and width a.
Classically that is the whole story: below V it bounces, every time;
above V it goes over, every time.  Quantum mechanically neither is true.
.PP
Below the barrier some of it gets through.  That is tunnelling, and it
is how alpha decay works and how a scanning tunnelling microscope sees
individual atoms.  Above the barrier some of it bounces back, which is
the more surprising half and has no classical counterpart at all.
.PP
Matching the wave and its slope at both faces gives, with
k = sqrt(2|E\-V|),
.PP
.EX
T = 4E|E-V| / ( 4E|E-V| + V^2 s )
R =     V^2 s / ( 4E|E-V| + V^2 s )
.EE
.PP
where s is sinh squared of ka below the barrier and sin squared of ka
above it.  Those are the same formula: sinh is what sin becomes when its
argument turns imaginary, which is exactly what happens to k when E
drops below V.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/tunnel
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script \(em the whole model is the text of
.BR /appl/danby/tunnel.spec .
The x axis is ENERGY, not time; the model sweeps E upward and evaluates
a formula.  The diagram shows the barrier itself with the energy drawn
across it, rising through the run.
.SS What to look for
.B The resonances.
Above the barrier, whenever ka is a whole number of pi the sine
vanishes and T is exactly 1: the barrier becomes perfectly transparent,
because the reflections from its two faces cancel.  Those energies are
.PP
.EX
E = V + n^2 pi^2 / 2a^2
.EE
.PP
This is the Ramsauer-Townsend effect, measured in the noble gases some
twenty years before anyone could explain it \(em slow electrons pass
through argon as though it were not there.
.PP
.B Tunnelling.
Set the energy below the barrier and T is small but not zero: at V = 10,
a = 1 and E = 2 it is 8.6e\-4.  Widen the barrier and it dies
exponentially \(em T goes as exp(\-2ka) once ka is large \(em so at
a = 2 the same energy gives 2.9e\-7, and at a = 2.5, 5.3e\-9.  That
steepness is why a tunnelling microscope can resolve a single atomic
step: a fraction of a nanometre changes the current by orders of
magnitude.
.PP
Widening the barrier also draws the resonances together, since they go
as 1 over a squared.  One slider does both.
.SS Conservation
.B T
and
.B R
are written out as two separate expressions, and not one as 1 minus the
other, so that T + R = 1 is a claim about both rather than a tautology
about one.  It is conservation of the particle \(em it has to end up
somewhere \(em and
.IR danbylabtest (1)
holds it to the last bit: the worst error over four thousand energies is
2.2e\-16.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/tunnel.spec
